      hubVsController: {
        id: 'hub-vs-controller',
        title: 'Hub vs Controller vs Coordinator vs Matter Controller vs AI Server — What Each Word Actually Means',
        content: "**These terms get used interchangeably in marketing copy, but they describe different jobs — knowing which is which prevents buying the wrong thing.**",
        items: [
          "Hub: the general term for the box that runs your automation logic and talks to your devices — Home Assistant Green, Hubitat, and Homey Pro are all hubs.",
          "Coordinator: the specific radio chip/software that manages a Zigbee mesh network — this is what a USB dongle like the Connect ZBT-2 provides for Home Assistant, or what's built into Hubitat and Homey.",
          "Controller (Matter controller): the software role that can commission and manage Matter devices — Home Assistant, Hubitat, Homey, Aqara Hub M3, and IKEA DIRIGERA can all act as Matter controllers, but with different device compatibility.",
          "Border router (Thread): a device that bridges the Thread mesh network to your regular Wi-Fi/Ethernet network — Homey Pro and IKEA DIRIGERA have one built in; Home Assistant Green needs a dongle for this role.",
          "AI server: a separate role entirely — the machine (or add-on) running a local LLM via Ollama. This can be the same box as your hub (one-box) or a dedicated machine the hub talks to (two-box) — see the next section.",
        ],
      },
      oneBoxTwoBox: {
        id: 'one-box-two-box',
        title: 'One-Box vs Two-Box Architecture',
        content: "**Should your hub and your local-AI server be the same machine? For casual setups, yes. For a serious local-AI-focused setup, this site's own hardware guide treats a dedicated AI box as the more resilient choice — worth doing the same here.**",
        items: [
          "One-box: a single mini PC running Home Assistant, Zigbee/Thread coordination, Frigate camera detection, and a local LLM via Ollama all on one machine. Cheap and simple, but every one of those jobs competes for the same RAM/CPU, and a crash or update to any one component can take down your whole smart home at once.",
          "Two-box: Home Assistant Green (or a similar lightweight hub) stays dedicated to hub duties and stays stable, while a separate, more powerful local-AI PC — see this site's mini PC guide, with the Beelink SER8 (~$650) as the current all-round pick — runs the heavier local-LLM workload and talks to the hub over the local network.",
          "Text-flow for two-box: [Home Assistant Green: devices + automations] → network → [AI mini PC: Ollama + LLM] → response → [Home Assistant Green: executes action].",
          "Why two-box is the more resilient architecture for local-AI-focused readers: a local LLM update, crash, or resource spike on the AI box never takes down basic lighting, locks, or security automations running on the hub — the hub keeps working even if the AI box is rebooting.",
          "Choose one-box if you're on a tight budget or a small/simple setup where downtime risk is low; choose two-box once you're running anything you'd genuinely miss if it went offline.",
        ],
      },
